 impact)", "History of gonococcal urethritis and high-risk sexual activity (possible gynecological pathology)", "Medications including ibuprofen and acetaminophen (GI irritation risk)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Septic shock: Absence of fever (99.5°F/37.5°C is borderline) and leukocytosis weakly disfavors this, though occult infection remains possible", "Metabolic crisis from rapid weight loss: Unlikely to explain haemodynamic instability without profound electrolyte derangements", "Alcoholic hepatitis with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis: Would typically present with ascites and fever, not isolated RUQ pain with shock", "Pancreatitis: Would more commonly present with epigastric pain radiating to back, not localized RUQ"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension with tachycardia (shock)", "Severe focal right upper quadrant tenderness", "Recent significant weight loss in a young woman", "High-dose ibuprofen use in context of hypotension"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic / Pelvic Cause of Acute Right Upper Quadrant Pain", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.222, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of adnexal signs weakly disfavors a gynecologic cause (B1) of shock, though high-risk behavior and RUQ pain maintain some plausibility."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Cause of Acute Right Upper Quadrant Pain", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.4, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Chronic alcohol use and weight loss provide moderate support for hepatic venous obstruction (B2), though absence of jaundice/ascites introduces some uncertaint…"], "evidence_against": ["Absence of jaundice and ascites weakly disfavors Budd-Chiari syndrome (B2), but chronic alcohol use and weight loss retain some suspicion."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.